@@ -404,6 +404,7 @@ scrape_configs:
 * `disable_keepalive: true` for disabling [HTTP keep-alive connections](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HTTP_persistent_connection)
   on a per-job basis. By default, vmagent uses keep-alive connections when scraping targets. This reduces overhead by eliminating the need to repeatedly reestablish connections.
 * `series_limit: N` for limiting the number of unique time series a single scrape target can expose. See [these docs](#cardinality-limiter).
+* `max_scrape_targets: N` for limiting the number of active scrape targets a single job can have at once, regardless of the service discovery mechanism(s) which provided the targets. See [these docs](#limiting-the-number-of-targets-per-job).
 * `stream_parse: true` for scraping targets in a streaming manner. This may be useful when targets export a large number of metrics. See [these docs](#stream-parsing-mode).
 * `scrape_align_interval: duration` for aligning scrapes to the given interval instead of using a random offset
   in the range `[0 ... scrape_interval]` for scraping each target. The random offset helps to spread scrapes evenly in time.
@@ -1125,6 +1126,31 @@ When the `-remoteWrite.disableOnDiskQueue` command-line flag is set, `vmagent` m
 if it cannot keep up with the data ingestion rate. In this case [deduplication](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/single-server-victoriametrics/#deduplication)
 must be enabled on all the configured remote storage systems.
 
+## Limiting the number of targets per job
+
+Service discovery mechanisms such as `kubernetes_sd_configs`, `consul_sd_configs`, `ec2_sd_configs`, etc.
+occasionally return a much bigger number of targets than expected because of a misconfiguration
+on the discovery side (for example, an accidentally removed tag filter or an overly broad catalog query).
+When this happens, `vmagent` may try to scrape all the returned targets at once, which can spike memory
+and CPU usage and degrade scraping for every other job running on the same `vmagent` instance.
+
+By default `vmagent` doesn't limit the number of active scrape targets a single job (`job_name`) can have.
+The limit can be enforced in the following places:
+
+* Via `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` command-line flag. This limit is applied by default
+  to all the jobs defined in the file pointed to by `-promscrape.config`.
+* Via `max_scrape_targets` config option at [scrape_config](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/#scrape_configs) section.
+  The `max_scrape_targets` allows overriding the `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` on a per-`scrape_config` basis.
+  If `max_scrape_targets` is set to `0` or to a negative value, then the `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` value is used for the job.
+
+The limit is applied uniformly to the combined set of targets discovered by the job, no matter which
+service discovery mechanism(s) provided them. When a job's discovered target count exceeds the configured
+limit, `vmagent` keeps scraping only up to `max_scrape_targets` targets for that job — it neither crashes
+nor rejects the whole job, and it doesn't silently scrape all the discovered targets either. The excluded
+targets are shown as dropped targets with reason `job's max_scrape_targets limit exceeded` at the
+`/targets` and `/service-discovery` pages, and at the `/api/v1/targets` API,
+so it is obvious which job(s) are currently over the limit and how many targets are being excluded because of it.
+
 ## Cardinality limiter
 
 By default, `vmagent` doesn't limit the number of time series each scrape target can expose.
